# Node command-line digest

Distilled from Node's `util`, `process` and `fs` docs, the Command Line
Interface Guidelines, the GNU coding standards, npm's `package.json` and
`npm pack` docs, esbuild's API and NO_COLOR (see `SOURCES.md`). `[lint: x]` =
enforced by `make check`; `[review]` = check it yourself.

## The command line

- Parse with `util.parseArgs` and `strict: true`: an unknown option or a value
  of the wrong type throws, and `run` turns that into a usage error. [review]
- Every option has a long name; a one-letter `short` only for the common
  ones. Use the usual names (`--verbose`, `--output`, `--force`). [review]
- `--help` and `--version` print to stdout and exit 0; other flags on the
  line are ignored. Help starts with a usage line and lists every flag. [review]
- Positionals are off by default under `strict`; turn on `allowPositionals`
  only when the program takes operands. Prefer flags to positionals. [review]
- Everything after `--` is a positional, even when it starts with `-`. A lone
  `-` is a positional too: read it as stdin, or stdout for an output path. [review]
- Boolean `--no-x` flags need `allowNegative` (Node 22.4); without it, define
  the negated flag yourself. [review]
- `values` from `parseArgs` is typed by the `options` object declared
  `as const`; positionals are `string[]`, so index them with a check. [lint: tsc noUncheckedIndexedAccess]

## Output and exit codes

- Results go to stdout, so they pipe; diagnostics, progress and warnings go to
  stderr. [review]
- `src/cli.ts` writes only through the `Output` it is given, never the
  console. [lint: no-console]
- `main.ts` sets `process.exitCode` and lets Node exit. `process.exit()` can
  drop writes still pending on a pipe. [lint: no-restricted-properties]
- Exit 0 on success (help and version too), 2 for a usage error, 1 for any
  other failure. Export the codes from `cli.ts` and test them. [review]
- Output for machines is stable and plain: one record per line, or `--json`
  when it is structured. No decoration when stdout is not a TTY. [review]
- Piped to `head`, writes to stdout fail with `EPIPE` once the reader is gone.
  That is the reader's choice, not a crash: handle `'error'` on stdout in
  `main.ts`, stop writing, and print no stack trace. [review]
- Say something within about 100 ms when work will take a while, on
  stderr. [review]

## Errors

- An expected error is one line on stderr: the program's name, what failed
  and the value involved (`greet: cannot read config.json: no such file`). [review]
- Say what to do next ("Run 'greet --help' for usage."). [review]
- No stack trace for expected errors: catch them and rewrite them for
  people. Keep traces for bugs, or behind `--verbose`. [review]
- Throw only `Error` objects; catch variables are `unknown`, so narrow them
  before reading `.message`. [lint: @typescript-eslint/only-throw-error; tsc strict]
- An uncaught exception exits 1 with a trace; don't count on it. [review]

## Input and files

- Use `node:fs/promises` and await it. Don't use sync calls on paths that
  read user data. [review]
- `readFile` buffers the whole file. For input that can be large, stream it
  (`createReadStream`, `readline`) and process it as it arrives. [review]
- Relative paths resolve against the current directory, as the user typed
  them; never against the program's own location. [review]
- Read and write text as UTF-8 and say so (`readFile(path, 'utf8')`). [review]
- File names are input files; output files come from a flag such as
  `-o`/`--output`. [review]
- Prompt only when stdin is a TTY, and give a flag for every prompt. Check
  `isTTY` on the stream involved. [review]
- No colour, spinners or animation when stdout is not a TTY, when `NO_COLOR`
  is set and not empty, or when `TERM=dumb`. A `--color` flag or config may
  override `NO_COLOR`. [review]
- `run` never touches `process`: `main.ts` passes in what it needs (env,
  stdin, `isTTY`, an `AbortSignal`), so tests can set it. [review]

## Signals and async

- Every promise is awaited or returned; none is left floating. [lint: @typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises]
- No async function where a callback must return `void`, such as an event
  listener. [lint: @typescript-eslint/no-misused-promises]
- When the program does I/O, `run` returns `Promise<number>` and `main.ts`
  awaits it before setting `exitCode`. [review]
- Ctrl-C sends `SIGINT`. Without a listener Node exits 130 (128 + 2) at once.
  A listener removes that default, so only add one to clean up. [review]
- On `SIGINT`, abort an `AbortController`, pass its signal to `fs` calls and
  timers, say on stderr that the program is stopping, and set exit code 130. [review]
- Cleanup has a time limit; a second Ctrl-C stops without it. [review]

## Configuration

- Precedence, highest first: flags, environment variables, a project config
  file, a user config file. [review]
- Never take a secret from a flag or an environment variable: they leak into
  `ps` and shell history. Read it from a file or stdin. [review]
- Never print a secret, in errors and `--verbose` output included. [review]

## Bundling and packaging

- esbuild bundles `src/main.ts` into one ES module, `out/cli.js`, for
  `node22`. With `platform: 'node'` Node's built-in modules stay external;
  import them as `node:fs`. [review]
- The banner adds the shebang that `bin` needs and a `require` from
  `createRequire`, for bundled CommonJS code that requires a built-in. [review]
- The bundle holds everything, so the package has no `dependencies`: every
  package goes in `devDependencies`. [review]
- Packages that read their own files at run time (`__dirname`,
  `import.meta.url`) or ship native `.node` modules may not bundle; pick
  another. [review]
- esbuild's `target` lowers syntax only, never adds APIs. `engines.node` is
  `>=22` and `@types/node` describes Node 24: use no Node API newer than
  Node 22. [review]
- `engines` only warns unless the user sets `engine-strict`. [review]
- `npm pack` runs `prepack`, which builds, and makes the release tarball.
  `files` keeps it to the bundle and the CHANGELOG, plus what npm always adds
  (`package.json`, README, LICENSE). [lint: make pack-check packs it and runs --version]
- `bin` names the command after the container. [review]
- `package.json`'s `version` is the release and what `--version` prints; each
  release has a CHANGELOG entry, and its tag matches the version. [lint: make dist refuses a mismatch; CHANGELOG review]

## Tests

- Tests call `run` with a recording `Output` and check the exit code,
  stdout and stderr together. No child process, no real terminal. [review]
- Cover each flag, `--help`, `--version`, an unknown flag (exit 2, a message
  on stderr, nothing on stdout) and each failure exit. [review]
- Files go in a temporary directory made in the test and removed after. [review]
- Exported functions declare their parameter and return types. [lint: @typescript-eslint/explicit-module-boundary-types]
- vitest runs in `make test`; `make pack-check` runs the packed program once. [lint: make check]
